  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist. The rule is enabled and reports status `ok`.\n\n**Data view findings** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, 14,074 requests)\n- Key fields: `response` (status code, text with a `.keyword` subfield), `clientip`, `bytes`, `geo.dest`, `extension`, `machine.os`, `agent`, `url`, and `timestamp`.\n- Status codes: `200` (12,832), `404` (801), `503` (441). The only 5xx code in the data is `503`.\n- The data spans 2026-09-27 to 2026-11-26, which runs past today's date (2026-10-10). I set the dashboard time range to 2026-09-01 through 2026-12-31 so all the data shows.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-9271eb0d Web logs overview\"** (`eff-9271eb0d-web-logs-overview-f0ec15fb396e`)\n1. Total requests (metric)\n2. Unique client IPs (metric)\n3. Requests over time (line, date histogram)\n4. Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n5. 5xx responses (ES|QL metric, `TO_INTEGER(response)` between 500 and 599)\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-9271eb0d 5xx spike\"** (`e65c04d1-cbe5-473d-9868-739849efd68f`)\n- Type `.es-query` in ES|QL mode, consumer `stackAlerts`, runs every `1m`, tagged `EFF-9271eb0d`.\n- Looks back over a 5-minute window and fires when more than 10 requests have a 5xx response.\n- The threshold is inside the query (`WHERE server_errors > 10`) because Kibana requires the rule's own threshold to be `0` for ES|QL rules.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- I enabled the rule. The user asked for a rule that checks every minute, and rules are created disabled by default. Disable it with `disable_alert_rule` if you want it off.\n- I added the `EFF-9271eb0d` tag to the rule so it's easy to find.\n- The 5xx metric uses ES|QL rather than the standard chart filter, because the filter only supports exact matches.\n\nI couldn't query Elasticsearch directly, so I verified the status-code counts through Kibana's internal ES|QL endpoint. I didn't see the rule fire. This sample data is static, so it won't produce a spike unless new 5xx traffic arrives in the window.",
